<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jesuit Missions of Moxos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1682">1682</a>–<a href="/years/1767">1767</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jesuit Missions of Mojos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Missions of Moxos were mission towns founded from 1682 among the Moxo and other peoples of the seasonally flooded plains of the Beni, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. They became a populous frontier of the Spanish empire facing Portuguese Mato Grosso across the Guaporé. After the Jesuits were expelled in 1767, the missions passed to secular administration and declined.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesuit-missions-of-moxos.jpg" alt="Jesuit Missions of Moxos" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franciscan_missionaries_in_California.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
